      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Abdominal tuberculosis (TB) continues to give rise to diagnostic and therapeutic challenges. A total of 24 patients with abdominal TB who presented to general surgeons over a 9-year period have been reviewed. Most (92 per cent) of these patients were Asian; only one had a past history of pulmonary TB. The most common presenting complaint was abdominal pain in 21 patients (88 per cent) with the associated symptoms of weight loss in 18 (75 per cent), anorexia in 15 (62 per cent) and night sweats in 13 (54 per cent). A tissue diagnosis was obtained in 18 patients (75 per cent) and 17 patients (71 per cent) underwent laparotomy. These results show that the diagnosis of abdominal TB is still difficult to establish, and that many patients undergo laparotomy despite the existence of less invasive diagnostic procedures."},
